🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗧𝗲𝗿𝗺𝗶𝗻𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗜𝗺𝗺𝗲𝗱𝗶𝗮𝘁𝗲𝗹𝘆 — 𝗪𝗵𝗮𝘁 𝗪𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗬𝗼𝘂 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “A Kubernetes pod starts successfully but then terminates within a few seconds. How will you troubleshoot it?”

I wouldn't immediately restart or redeploy the pod.

First, I would identify why the container is exiting.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄

1️⃣ Check pod status

kubectl get pods -n <namespace>

Look at the STATUS and RESTARTS columns.

2️⃣ Describe the pod

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

Check the Events and container state.

Look for:

🔹 Exit codes
🔹 OOMKilled
🔹 Probe failures
🔹 Mount/configuration errors
🔹 Container termination reasons

3️⃣ Check application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace>

If the container has already restarted:

kubectl logs <pod-name> --previous -n <namespace>

The previous logs can reveal why the last container instance exited.

4️⃣ Check the container command

I would verify the image's:

🔹 command
🔹 args
🔹 Environment variables
🔹 ConfigMaps/Secrets

A container can terminate immediately if its main process finishes or the startup command is incorrect.

5️⃣ Check the exit code

The exit code can provide an important clue about how the process terminated.

kubectl describe pod <pod-name> -n <namespace>

🎯 𝗧𝗵𝗲 𝗙𝗹𝗼𝘄 𝗜 𝗙𝗼𝗹𝗹𝗼𝘄:

Pod Status → Container State → Events → Previous Logs → Exit Code → Command/Args → Root Cause

𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗹𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴:

A container being Running briefly doesn't mean the application is healthy.

The most important question is:

👉 Why did the main process exit?

Find the reason first, then take the recovery action. 🚀
